WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.160
A nucleotide is one of the most expensive small molecules a cell makes.

2
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:13.980
Nucleotides are the letters of DNA and RNA, and the carriers of energy, ATP and GTP.

3
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.260
They form the cores of signaling molecules like cyclic AMP.

4
00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:25.580
They also form the backbones of cofactors such as NAD⁺, +, FAD, and coenzyme A.

5
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:33.533
Building

6
00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:37.813
There are two routes to a purine nucleotide, and the difference is mostly about

7
00:00:37.813 --> 00:00:43.513
cost. De novo synthesis builds the purine ring atom by atom, directly onto an

8
00:00:43.513 --> 00:00:46.033
activated ribose called PRPP.

9
00:00:46.953 --> 00:00:51.933
It runs about 10 enzymatic steps and spends roughly 6 ATP to make the first finished

10
00:00:51.933 --> 00:00:53.833
purine nucleotide, IMP.

11
00:00:54.513 --> 00:00:57.093
Both AMP and GMP branch from IMP.

12
00:00:57.983 --> 00:01:03.443
Salvage takes a free base that already exists, such as hypoxanthine, guanine, or

13
00:01:03.443 --> 00:01:07.843
adenine, and reattaches it to PRPP in a single step.

14
00:01:08.523 --> 00:01:13.363
The enzyme HGPRT salvages hypoxanthine and guanine.

15
00:01:13.763 --> 00:01:15.663
a PRT salvages adenine.

16
00:01:16.203 --> 00:01:20.323
Recycling is so much cheaper that cells recover something like 90% of their free

17
00:01:20.323 --> 00:01:22.523
purines. Salvage is the default.

18
00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:25.103
Now imagine the recycling is gone.

19
00:01:25.843 --> 00:01:31.523
In Lesch-Nyhan syndrome, an X-linked defect, HGPRT is missing.

20
00:01:32.223 --> 00:01:36.763
Free bases that should have been reclaimed are dumped into the catabolic pathway and

21
00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:38.143
turned into uric acid.

22
00:01:38.763 --> 00:01:43.743
The PRPP that salvage would have used piles up and pushes de novo synthesis even

23
00:01:43.743 --> 00:01:46.903
higher. That makes still more purines to degrade.

24
00:01:46.903 --> 00:01:52.163
The result is severe hyperuricemia alongside profound neurological disease.

25
00:01:53.083 --> 00:01:57.303
When a purine is finally retired, it is taken apart down a short tail.

26
00:01:57.863 --> 00:02:01.943
AMP and adenosine funnel through inosine to hypoxanthine.

27
00:02:02.323 --> 00:02:08.663
Then the enzyme xanthine oxidase oxidizes hypoxanthine to xanthine, and xanthine to

28
00:02:08.663 --> 00:02:13.183
uric acid. Guanine joins the route one step later, at xanthine.

29
00:02:13.763 --> 00:02:19.403
Most mammals carry uricase, which converts uric acid into highly soluble allantoin.

30
00:02:20.123 --> 00:02:24.243
Humans and the other great apes lost a working uricase during evolution.

31
00:02:25.103 --> 00:02:29.863
For us, uric acid is the end product, and it is only sparingly soluble.

32
00:02:30.733 --> 00:02:33.753
That single missing enzyme is why we get gout.

33
00:02:34.333 --> 00:02:40.033
Once serum urate climbs past roughly 6.8 milligrams per deciliter, it begins to

34
00:02:40.033 --> 00:02:42.313
crystallize as monosodium urate.

35
00:02:42.713 --> 00:02:45.333
Those needle-sharp crystals in a joint are the flare.

36
00:02:45.873 --> 00:02:47.473
The fix is to make less of it.

37
00:02:48.073 --> 00:02:53.313
Allopurinol, a hypoxanthine look-alike, inhibits xanthine oxidase, so urate

38
00:02:53.313 --> 00:02:55.633
production falls and the crystals can dissolve.

39
00:02:56.333 --> 00:03:00.273
Gertrude Elion and George Hitchings designed it by reasoning about the enzyme.
